1603:
(a) The Mayor may declare a State of Local Emergency under the authority granted in Section 9-4.
(b) When a state of emergency is declared, the Mayor shall exercise all powers granted to him or her under state law and under the Macon-Bibb County Charter and, in doing so, may waive any requirement of any law or rule that impedes the emergency efforts being undertaken by the county. Specifically, the Mayor may:
(1) Delegate his or her authority to the County Manager or Emergency Management Director;
(2) Issue evacuation and curfew orders;
(3) Call emergency meetings of the County Commission;
(4) Issue orders necessary for the protection of life and property, establishment of public order, and control of adverse conditions affecting public welfare resulting from an emergency, and implement orders issued by federal, state or county emergency management agencies;
(5) Suspend or limit the sale, dispensing or transportation of alcoholic beverages, explosives and or combustibles; provided, however, that for purposes of this paragraph, the terms "explosives" and "combustibles" shall not include firearms or ammunition or any component thereof;
(6) Direct and compel the evacuation of all or part of the population from any stricken or threatened area, for the preservation of life or other disaster mitigation, response or recovery;
(7) Authorize the use of public property when necessary to cope with the emergency or disaster or when there is compelling necessity for the protection of lives, health, and welfare, or the property of citizens and inhabitants;
(8) Perform such other functions, powers, and duties as may be deemed necessary to promote and secure the safety and protection of the civilian population, including individuals with household pets and service animals prior to, during, and following a major disaster or emergency;
(9) Enter into mutual aid agreements with jurisdictions offering assistance to Macon-Bibb County for response and recovery to the specific critical event; and
(10) Exercise all powers granted in O.C.G.A. §§ 38-3-27 through 38-3-28 and 38-3-54 through 38-3-56, as amended.
(c) Chain of Succession. In the event that the Mayor of Macon-Bibb County Government is unavailable during an emergency or disaster, pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 38-3-50(f) the following elected officials shall be appointed as the Chief Elected Official during the emergency event in descending order of succession:
(1) Mayor;
(2) Mayor Pro-Tem;
(3) The third commissioner serving on the Committee on Committees.
(Ord. No. O-21-0048, § 1, 6-15-2021)

1604:
The County Manager shall:
(a) Exercise all powers delegated to him or her by the Mayor, when a declaration of state of emergency is issued, and take all prudent and lawful actions necessary to protect the health, safety and welfare of the community and the public;
(b) Coordinate with the Mayor, EMA Director, and other governmental entities to ensure the County's actions are consistent with federal, state and county emergency management plans;
(c) Utilize all available county resources as reasonably necessary to cope with the emergency or disaster to include relieving employees of normal duties and temporarily reassign them to other emergency or disaster-related duties;
(d) Apply for public assistance or other disaster relief funding;
(e) At the direction of the Mayor, expend county funds as necessary to cope with the emergency up to the amount contained in the Emergency Management Immediate Response Fund;
(f) At the Mayor's direction, enter into contracts addressing situations arising out of the emergency;
(g) Provide benefits to citizens and inhabitants upon execution of an intergovernmental agreement for grants to meet disaster-related necessary expenses or serious needs of individuals or families adversely affected by an emergency or disaster in cases where the individuals or families are unable to meet the expenses or needs from other means, provided that such grants are authorized only when matching state or federal funds are available for such purposes;
(h) Perform and exercise such other functions, powers, and duties as may be deemed necessary to promote and secure the safety and protection of the civilian population, including individuals with household pets and service animals prior to, during, and following a major disaster or emergency; and
(i) If a state of disaster or emergency is declared by the governor, assign and make available for duty the employees, property, or equipment of the county within or outside the geographical limits of the county as ordered by the governor.
(Ord. No. O-21-0048, § 1, 6-15-2021)

1613:
(a) Institution of curfew.
(1) Upon the declaration of a state of emergency by the governor, or a declaration of a local state of emergency, the Mayor may order a curfew is in effect when it is determined to be necessary to protect and safeguard the people and property of Macon-Bibb County.
(2) All of the territory of Macon-Bibb County shall be subject to the terms of the curfew, unless otherwise specified in the order implementing the curfew.
(3) The order implementing the curfew shall include the dates and hours that the curfew shall be in effect.
(b) Unlawful to violate curfew. To preserve, protect or sustain the life, health, welfare or safety of persons, or their property, within a designated area under a declaration of emergency, it shall be unlawful for any person to travel, loiter, wander, or stroll in or upon the public streets, highways, roads, lanes, parks or other public grounds, public places, public buildings, places of amusement, eating places, vacant lots, or any other place during a declared state of emergency between hours specified by the order implementing the curfew until the curfew is lifted.
(c) Authority to impose. To promote order, protect lives, minimize the potential for looting and other crimes, and facilitate recovery operations during a state of emergency, the Mayor, or his or her designee, shall have discretion to impose re-entry restrictions on certain areas. The Mayor, or his or her designee, shall exercise such discretion in accordance with the County's Emergency Operations Plan, which shall be followed during states of emergency.
(d) Exceptions. The provisions of this section shall not apply to persons acting in the following capacities:
1. Authorized and essential law enforcement personnel;
2. Authorized and essential health care providers;
3. Authorized and essential personnel of the county;
4. Authorized National Guard or federal military personnel;
5. Authorized and essential firefighters;
6. Authorized and essential emergency response personnel;
7. Authorized and essential personnel or volunteers working with or through the county;
8. Authorized and essential utility repair crews;
9. Persons seeking to restore order to their homes, businesses, or places of abode while on their own property or place of business or abode; and
10. Other authorized and essential persons as designated on a list compiled by the county.
(e) Enforceability. This section shall be enforced by officers of the law enforcement personnel approved to provide aid and assistance during the emergency. Nothing contained in this section shall prohibit a law enforcement officer from bringing other charges under state law.
(f) Effective date. This section shall become effective only upon the declaration of a State of Emergency by the President of the United States, the Governor of Georgia, or the declaration of a Local State of Emergency by the Mayor.
(Ord. No. O-21-0048, § 1, 6-15-2021)

